            <ArticleTitle>Lumbar artery pseudoaneurysm as a late complication of osteomyelitis with vertebral body destruction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1084-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jvs.2012.12.070</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0741-5214(13)00129-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 79-year-old patient whose acute back pain revealed a 9-cm nonruptured lumbar artery pseudoaneurysm occurring 5 years after a vertebral body osteomyelitis that induced a significant vertebral destruction. She underwent an urgent percutaneous selective arterial embolization. Lumbar artery false aneurysm should, thus, be suspected as a possible etiology of any new painful symptoms in patients suffering from a chronic vertebral destructive disease.</AbstractText>
